1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:03,400
Once upon a time in ancient China,

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,340
there lived a legendary archer named Hou Yi

3
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:09,480
and his wife, Chang'e.

4
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:11,860
Hou Yi was a hero.

5
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:16,120
When ten blazing suns once scorched the Earth,

6
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:19,800
he shot down nine of them and saved the people.

7
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:22,320
For this heroic deed,

8
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:26,720
the Queen Mother of the West gave him an
extraordinary reward:

9
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:31,640
a magical medicine called the elixir of immortality.

10
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:35,340
Whoever drank it would become an immortal —

11
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:40,620
a magical being who could live forever beyond the
mortal world.

12
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:44,000
But there was enough for only one person.

13
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:47,640
If Hou Yi drank it and became immortal,

14
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:50,940
he would have to leave the human world behind.

15
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:54,080
And that meant leaving Chang'e behind.

16
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:57,260
So he chose not to drink it.

17
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:01,580
Instead, he asked Chang'e to keep the elixir safe.

18
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:05,200
But one day, while Hou Yi was away,

19
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:10,020
one of his apprentices, Pang Meng, tried to steal
it.

20
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:12,680
Chang'e couldn’t let him have it.

21
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:16,740
With no time to lose, she made a desperate choice.

22
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:19,020
She drank it herself.

23
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,460
Almost at once, she began to rise.

24
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,780
The elixir had made her immortal!

25
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:29,580
She could no longer remain in the human world.

26
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:32,580
Higher and higher she floated —

27
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:37,700
above the house, above the mountains, and into the
night sky.

28
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:41,040
But Chang'e still loved Hou Yi.

29
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:44,760
She wanted to stay as close to him as she could.

30
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:47,360
So she chose the Moon —

31
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,780
the nearest place in the heavens to the human world
below.

32
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:56,580
When Hou Yi returned and learned what had happened,

33
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:58,520
he was heartbroken.

34
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:03,260
That night, he looked up at the bright full moon

35
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:05,440
and thought of Chang'e.

36
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:10,280
He set out fruits and cakes beneath the moonlight,

37
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:14,100
as if he could still share them with her from far
away.

38
00:02:14,140 --> 00:02:18,760
And that was how the Mid-Autumn Festival began.

39
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:20,580
The end.

40
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:22,580
Wait.

41
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:25,980
Is that really where the Mid-Autumn Festival came
from?

42
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:27,660
Well...

43
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:29,380
That’s the legend.

44
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:32,820
Real history is a little messier.

45
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:35,280
Festivals aren’t always invented.

46
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:37,000
Sometimes, they grow.

47
00:02:41,310 --> 00:02:43,330
Mid-Autumn grew from several old traditions

48
00:02:43,370 --> 00:02:45,110
that slowly came together.

49
00:02:45,690 --> 00:02:47,630
For most of Chinese history,

50
00:02:47,970 --> 00:02:50,110
people’s lives depended heavily on farming.

51
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:52,430
They had to watch the seasons carefully —

52
00:02:52,830 --> 00:02:55,570
when to plant, when the rains were likely to come,

53
00:02:55,670 --> 00:02:57,530
and when crops were ready to harvest.

54
00:02:58,330 --> 00:03:00,290
So autumn, the harvest season,

55
00:03:00,610 --> 00:03:03,590
naturally became a time to celebrate what had been
gathered,

56
00:03:04,090 --> 00:03:07,630
perform seasonal rituals, and pray for good harvests
to come.

57
00:03:08,130 --> 00:03:09,130
But why the Moon?

58
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:14,950
Ancient Chinese people kept track of time by
watching both the Sun and the Moon.

59
00:03:15,390 --> 00:03:17,710
The traditional Chinese calendar followed both.

60
00:03:18,670 --> 00:03:21,550
The Sun helped keep the calendar aligned with the
seasons.

61
00:03:22,070 --> 00:03:23,430
The Moon shaped the months.

62
00:03:24,410 --> 00:03:26,030
Around the middle of each month,

63
00:03:26,250 --> 00:03:27,750
the Moon becomes full.

64
00:03:28,410 --> 00:03:32,170
And long before the Mid-Autumn Festival existed in
the form we know today,

65
00:03:32,590 --> 00:03:36,490
people in China were already holding autumn rituals
to honour the Moon.

66
00:03:36,910 --> 00:03:40,050
Over many centuries, harvest traditions,

67
00:03:40,770 --> 00:03:44,810
rituals honouring the Moon, moon-gazing, poetry,
food,

68
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:48,350
and stories like Chang'e gradually came together.

69
00:03:49,170 --> 00:03:51,310
By the Tang and Song dynasties,

70
00:03:51,590 --> 00:03:55,590
something recognisably like today’s Mid-Autumn
Festival had taken shape.

71
00:03:56,250 --> 00:04:01,430
So no — the festival probably did not begin because
Chang'e flew to the Moon.

72
00:04:01,550 --> 00:04:03,250
But her story stayed,

73
00:04:03,290 --> 00:04:06,530
because it captured something people felt deeply.

74
00:04:07,090 --> 00:04:10,250
And that feeling would become the heart of
Mid-Autumn.

75
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:14,530
So what does Mid-Autumn actually look like today?

76
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:21,360
First, people come home.

77
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:23,420
More than anything else,

78
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:26,420
Mid-Autumn is a festival of reunion.

79
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:28,420
And in Chinese culture,

80
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:31,700
family is often thought of across generations,

81
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:33,460
not just as one household.

82
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:39,340
That can mean grandparents, parents, grown-up
children, brothers and sisters —

83
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:42,700
sometimes aunts, uncles, and cousins too.

84
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:46,680
Several generations come back to the same table.

85
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:50,180
And once they do, a pattern starts to appear.

86
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:53,360
The same shape keeps showing up.

87
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:55,080
A full moon.

88
00:04:55,740 --> 00:04:57,260
A round mooncake.

89
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:01,680
A family gathered in a circle around the dinner
table.

90
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:05,800
In Chinese, yuan means round.

91
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:10,560
And roundness carries the idea of being whole,
complete.

92
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:15,780
And that same yuan appears in tuanyuan — reunion:

93
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:19,700
people who belong together, together again.

94
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:22,260
A full moon is complete.

95
00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:24,140
No piece is missing.

96
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:27,780
And a family feels complete in much the same way:

97
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:29,780
when no one is missing.

98
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:34,840
That is why the full moon became such a powerful
symbol of reunion.

99
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:39,100
And that idea of reunion is everywhere in the
festival.

100
00:05:39,740 --> 00:05:42,020
Families sit down for a reunion dinner.

101
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:45,060
They cut and share round mooncakes.

102
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:47,940
And when night falls, they go outside —

103
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:52,120
or simply look out of a window — to admire the full
moon.

104
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:54,480
In many parts of China,

105
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:58,520
Mid-Autumn also arrives with the scent of osmanthus
—

106
00:05:58,780 --> 00:06:01,600
tiny golden flowers that bloom in autumn.

107
00:06:02,260 --> 00:06:03,360
People admire them,

108
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:05,620
flavour sweets and tea with them,

109
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:08,780
and traditionally even make osmanthus wine.

110
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:11,660
And China is a big place,

111
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:14,540
so different regions have traditions of their own.

112
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:16,380
In some parts of China,

113
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:19,980
children may carry lanterns or solve lantern
riddles.

114
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:26,500
In Shanghai, Mid-Autumn tables traditionally include
duck, taro, and edamame.

115
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:31,280
And in Hong Kong, a great fire dragon dances through
the streets

116
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:33,740
in a blaze of light and incense.

117
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:38,780
But Mid-Autumn isn’t only about the people who made
it home.

118
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:42,060
It is also about the ones who couldn’t.

119
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,880
For centuries, people have spent Mid-Autumn

120
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:48,160
far away from those they love —

121
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:52,600
away from parents, grandparents, children,

122
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:55,240
or the place they think of as home.

123
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:57,660
And when that happens,

124
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:00,220
people look up at the Moon.

125
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:02,840
Because somewhere far away,

126
00:07:03,260 --> 00:07:07,000
the people they love are under that same Moon.

127
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:11,820
The Moon becomes more than something beautiful in
the sky.

128
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:14,340
It becomes a way to remember.

129
00:07:15,340 --> 00:07:16,680
A way to miss someone.

130
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:21,500
A way to feel close across a great distance.

131
00:07:22,220 --> 00:07:26,360
And maybe that is why the story of Chang'e never
disappeared.

132
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:30,040
The legend probably did not create the festival.

133
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:32,740
But perhaps it stayed with the festival

134
00:07:32,780 --> 00:07:37,100
because, at its heart, it is also a story about
separation —

135
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:40,440
and the wish to be together again.

136
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:42,700
Hou Yi on Earth.

137
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:44,760
Chang'e on the Moon.

138
00:07:45,860 --> 00:07:48,620
Far apart, and still looking toward one another.

139
00:07:49,080 --> 00:07:52,360
The full moon is whole because nothing is missing.

140
00:07:53,180 --> 00:07:55,680
A family feels whole for the same reason.

141
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:58,320
And when someone is missing,

142
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:00,960
we look up, remember them,

143
00:08:01,140 --> 00:08:04,080
and know that the same Moon shines over them too.

144
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:10,660
月圆，人团圆。
When the Moon is full, may the family be whole.

145
00:08:11,060 --> 00:08:13,600
And wherever the people you love are,

146
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:16,320
may you be together again soon.

147
00:08:17,300 --> 00:08:21,090
Happy Mid-Autumn Festival.
中秋快乐！
